/* ============================================
   Color Palette – chromatic scales
   ============================================

   Nineteen accent scales built on the same conventions as the gray above:
   Radix hue-equivalents re-fit in OKLCH with a constant hue per scale (the
   chroma-weighted center of each Radix ramp's drift) and one shared lightness
   backbone across every scale, so any two colors at the same step have
   identical luminosity. Steps 1-8 of the dark scales carry the same
   darkening the gray applied to slate, keeping tinted surfaces on our
   elevation ladder.

   Steps 9/10 (solids) are the exception to the shared backbone: uniform
   solid lightness is a fallacy (yellow at blue's lightness is mustard), so
   solid lightness follows a smooth hill around the hue wheel — mid solids
   through the reds / greens / blues / purples, grading up through orange,
   lime, mint and sky to a peak at yellow. Step 9 is the same color in light
   and dark mode; step 10 shifts away from the mode's background for hover.

   Brand is anchored to the existing brand color at step 9 —
   oklch(0.649 0.247 9) — with crimson's ramp character around it.

   --{color}-bold is an extra emphasis stop for charts and other places a
   solid must stay assertive: step 9 is the same color in both modes, so it
   reads bold on white but soft against a dark backdrop. In light mode bold
   aliases step 9; in dark mode it rides the hue's chroma ceiling. On the
   red/blue side, where depth buys richness, it cuts 0.05 below step 9; in
   the green quadrant (lime through teal) the sRGB ceiling rises with
   lightness — darker only mutes — so bold instead climbs to step-10
   lightness (lime holds step 9, already atop the solid hill) and takes the
   bigger ceiling there. No alpha twin — it is a solid-only stop.

   Chroma is likewise non-uniform by design: each hue sits near its natural
   vividness for the step, soft-knee compressed toward (never onto) its sRGB
   gamut ceiling at the backbone lightness, so cross-hue chroma stays a
   smooth curve instead of kinking where a hard clamp would bite. The cyan
   quadrant (mint, teal, turquoise, sky) is the poorest region of sRGB, so
   those scales run hard against their ceiling — over-driven solids, higher
   solid lightness, and driven dark backgrounds — to read as vivid as their
   gamut-rich neighbors. A @media (color-gamut: p3) block at the end
   re-fits the ceiling-pinned steps against Display P3 for wide-gamut
   screens. There are no 0 / half steps here — those are gray-only
   extensions for the surface elevation ladder.

   Alpha scales: compositing --{color}-aN over white (light mode) or black
   (dark mode) reproduces --{color}-N; over --gray-1 the difference is
   sub-perceptual. Like the gray, none of these are exposed to Tailwind —
   semantic tokens should derive from them. */

